.0\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#0a3f52\"/><text x=\"390.0\" y=\"26.0\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#D4622A\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" >Even multiplicity (e.g. 2)</text><text x=\"390.0\" y=\"235.0\" font-size=\"13.5\" fill=\"#D4622A\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" >TOUCHES and turns around</text></svg></div><p class=\"graph-caption\">At an odd-multiplicity zero the graph passes straight through, like a line. At an even-multiplicity zero it behaves like a parabola — it touches the axis and bounces back on the same side.</p><div class=\"example-box\"><div class=\"label\">Example</div><p class=\"math-line\">In \\( f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)(x-3)^2 \\), the zero \\( x=3 \\) has multiplicity 2, because \\( (x-3) \\) appears twice.</p></div>"
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-4",
      "category": "conceptual",
      "question": "What is arrow notation, and how do I read or write it?",
      "answer": "Arrow notation is shorthand for a trend rather than an exact value — it pairs what x is doing with what f(x) is doing in response. It takes two separate statements, one for each direction, to describe both ends of a graph.<div class=\"graph-wrap\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 440 182\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"10\" width=\"380\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e6f2f2\" stroke=\"#1a6b6b\" stroke-width=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"24.0\" y=\"40.0\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#0a3f52\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"start\" >As x → ∞,  f(x) → ∞</text><text x=\"24.0\" y=\"66.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#4a4a5a\" font-weight=\"600\" text-anchor=\"start\" >\"as x gets larger and larger (heads right),</text><text x=\"24.0\" y=\"82.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#4a4a5a\" font-weight=\"600\" text-anchor=\"start\" >the graph's height also grows without bound (rises)\"</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"96\" width=\"380\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fdf0e8\" stroke=\"#D4622A\" stroke-width=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"24.0\" y=\"126.0\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#D4622A\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"start\" >As x → −∞,  f(x) → ∞</text><text x=\"24.0\" y=\"152.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#4a4a5a\" font-weight=\"600\" text-anchor=\"start\" >\"as x gets more and more negative (heads left),</text><text x=\"24.0\" y=\"168.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#4a4a5a\" font-weight=\"600\" text-anchor=\"start\" >the graph's height still grows without bound (rises)\"</text></svg></div><p class=\"graph-caption\">Two separate statements, one for each direction of x. Together they describe both ends at once — here, both ends rise.</p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-5",
      "category": "conceptual",
      "question": "What's the maximum number of turning points a polynomial can have, and how does that relate to its degree?",
      "answer": "A polynomial of degree \\( n \\) can have at most \\( n-1 \\) turning points (local maxima and minima combined) — it may have fewer, but never more. A degree-4 polynomial like \\( f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)(x-3)^2 \\), for instance, can turn at most 3 times, and it actually does: a local min, a local max, and then another local min (faq-10)."
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-6",
      "category": "sticking-points",
      "question": "How do I determine end behavior from the degree and leading coefficient?",
      "answer": "Find the degree (the highest exponent) and the sign of the leading coefficient (the number in front of that highest-degree term), then match them to one of four patterns.<div class=\"example-box\"><div class=\"label\">The Four Patterns</div><p class=\"math-line\">Even degree, positive leading coefficient: both ends point UP</p><p class=\"math-line\">Even degree, negative leading coefficient: both ends point DOWN</p><p class=\"math-line\">Odd degree, positive leading coefficient: down on the left, up on the right</p><p class=\"math-line\">Odd degree, negative leading coefficient: up on the left, down on the right</p></div>"
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-7",
      "category": "sticking-points",
      "question": "How do I find the y-intercept of a polynomial function?",
      "answer": "Evaluate the function at \\( x=0 \\) — substitute 0 in for every x and simplify. In factored form, this often means multiplying together the constant terms left over from each factor.<div class=\"example-box\"><div class=\"label\">Worked Example</div><p class=\"math-line\">\\( f(0) = (0-2)(0+1)(0-3)^2 = (-2)(1)(9) = -18 \\), so the y-intercept is \\( (0,-18) \\).</p></div>"
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-8",
      "category": "sticking-points",
      "question": "How do I tell whether a graph crosses or touches the x-axis at a zero?",
      "answer": "Check whether that zero's multiplicity is odd or even. Odd multiplicity (1, 3, 5, …) means the graph crosses straight through. Even multiplicity (2, 4, 6, …) means the graph touches the axis and turns back around, staying on the same side."
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-9",
      "category": "sticking-points",
      "question": "How does an even-multiplicity zero relate to a local max or min?",
      "answer": "An even-multiplicity zero IS a local extremum — the exact point where the graph touches the axis and turns around is, by definition, a turning point. Whether it's a local max or a local min depends on which side the rest of the curve sits on: if the curve approaches from above and turns back up, the touch point is a local minimum (value 0, the lowest point nearby); if the curve approaches from below and turns back down, it's a local maximum.<div class=\"example-box\"><div class=\"label\">Two Examples</div><p class=\"math-line\">In \\( f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)(x-3)^2 \\), the curve dips up toward \\( x=3 \\) from below and turns back down, so \\( x=3 \\) is a local <strong>minimum</strong> — even though it sits close to a much deeper minimum nearby, it's still its own separate turning point.</p><p class=\"math-line\">In \\( g(x)=(x+1)^2(x-5) \\), the curve rises up to \\( x=-1 \\) from below and turns back down, making \\( x=-1 \\) a local <strong>maximum</strong>.</p></div>"
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-10",
      "category": "sticking-points",
      "question": "How do I sketch a full polynomial graph from its factored form?",
      "answer": "Work through the features in order: find every zero and its multiplicity (to know where the graph meets the axis and how it behaves there), find the y-intercept, determine the end behavior from the degree and leading coefficient, and estimate roughly where any turning points fall between the zeros. Then connect everything with a smooth curve.<div class=\"graph-wrap\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 490 300\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\"><line x1=\"44.0\" y1=\"66.0\" x2=\"396.0\" y2=\"66.0\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" /><line x1=\"165.0\" y1=\"31.2\" x2=\"165.0\" y2=\"286.4\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" /><polyline points=\"46.8,-1402.3 48.2,-1345.0 49.6,-1289.0 51.1,-1234.4 52.5,-1181.1 54.0,-1129.1 55.4,-1078.4 56.9,-1029.0 58.3,-980.8 59.8,-933.9 61.2,-888.1 62.7,-843.6 64.1,-800.2 65.6,-758.0 67.0,-716.9 68.5,-677.0 69.9,-638.1 71.4,-600.3 72.8,-563.6 74.3,-528.0 75.7,-493.3 77.2,-459.7 78.6,-427.1 80.1,-395.4 81.5,-364.8 83.0,-335.0 84.4,-306.2 85.9,-278.3 87.3,-251.3 88.8,-225.2 90.2,-200.0 91.7,-175.6 93.1,-152.0 94.6,-129.3 96.0,-107.3 97.5,-86.1 98.9,-65.8 100.4,-46.1 101.8,-27.2 103.3,-9.0 104.7,8.4 106.2,25.2 107.6,41.3 109.1,56.7 110.5,71.4 112.0,85.6 113.4,99.0 114.9,111.9 116.3,124.2 117.8,135.9 119.2,147.0 120.7,157.6 122.1,167.6 123.6,177.1 125.0,186.1 126.5,194.6 127.9,202.5 129.4,210.0 130.8,217.1 132.3,223.6 133.7,229.7 135.2,235.4 136.6,240.7 138.1,245.5 139.5,250.0 141.0,254.1 142.4,257.8 143.9,261.1 145.3,264.1 146.8,266.7 148.2,269.0 149.7,271.0 151.1,272.6 152.6,274.0 154.0,275.1 155.5,275.9 156.9,276.4 158.4,276.7 159.8,276.7 161.3,276.4 162.7,276.0 164.2,275.3 165.6,274.4 167.1,273.3 168.5,272.0 170.0,270.5 171.4,268.8 172.9,267.0 174.3,264.9 175.8,262.8 177.2,260.5 178.7,258.0 180.1,255.4 181.6,252.7 183.0,249.9 184.5,247.0 185.9,244.0 187.4,240.8 188.8,237.6 190.3,234.3 191.7,230.9 193.2,227.5 194.6,224.0 196.1,220.5 197.5,216.9 199.0,213.2 200.4,209.5 201.9,205.8 203.3,202.1 204.8,198.3 20