    {
      "id": "faq-11",
      "category": "sticking-points",
      "question": "How do I read zeros, multiplicities, intercepts, extrema, and end behavior straight off a graph?",
      "answer": "Read each feature directly from what the picture shows: the zeros are wherever the curve touches the x-axis, whether it crosses (odd multiplicity) or bounces (even multiplicity) tells you the multiplicity's parity, the y-intercept is where the curve crosses the y-axis, the local maxes and mins are the hills and valleys, and the end behavior is simply which way the two ends point. The graph in faq-10 is a good one to practice on: try reading off every feature yourself before checking it against the worked answer."
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-12",
      "category": "sticking-points",
      "question": "How do I build a polynomial's equation from a description of its zeros, multiplicities, and end behavior?",
      "answer": "Write one factor per zero, using an exponent to match each stated multiplicity, then check that the resulting degree and leading coefficient actually produce the end behavior you were given — adjusting the sign out front if they don't yet match.<div class=\"example-box\"><div class=\"label\">Worked Example</div><p class=\"math-line\">Zeros at 0 and 2 (even multiplicity) and 5 (odd multiplicity), with both ends rising: try \\( f(x)=x^2(x-2)^2(x-5) \\).</p><p class=\"math-line\">This has degree 5 (odd) — but odd-degree graphs have their ends pointing in OPPOSITE directions, which doesn't match \"both ends rise.\"</p><p class=\"math-line\">Bump the multiplicity at 5 to make the degree even instead: \\( f(x)=x^2(x-2)^2(x-5)^2 \\) has degree 6 (even) with a positive leading coefficient, giving both ends up — but this changes 5's multiplicity to even, contradicting \\\"5 has odd multiplicity.\\\"</p><p class=\"math-line\">The two conditions can't both be satisfied by this zero set alone — a check like this can catch specifications that are actually inconsistent, or reveal that an extra even-multiplicity factor (with no new real zero) is needed to fix the degree's parity.</p></div>"
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-13",
      "category": "connections",
      "question": "How does this connect to PR1/PR2 (quadratic graphing)?",
      "answer": "A quadratic is just a degree-2 polynomial, so everything here is a direct generalization of what PR1/PR2 already taught. A quadratic's two zeros are this section's zeros; its one vertex is one of this section's (at most \\( n-1 \\)) turning points; and \\\"opens up or down\\\" is exactly this section's even-degree end behavior rule, applied to the one specific case of degree 2."
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-14",
      "category": "connections",
      "question": "How does multiplicity connect to factoring skills from earlier algebra?",
      "answer": "Multiplicity is nothing more than a repeated factor, which is exactly what you learned to spot when factoring expressions like \\( x^2-6x+9=(x-3)^2 \\) or \\( x^3-x^2=x^2(x-1) \\). Every factoring technique that produces a repeated factor is quietly telling you about a graph's behavior at that zero, even outside of a graphing context."
    },
    {
      "id": "faq-15",
      "category": "connections",
      "question": "Why do zeros, multiplicity, and end behavior matter beyond this course?",
      "answer": "Polynomial models show up constantly in engineering, economics, and physics — projectile paths, cost and revenue curves, and signal shapes are all polynomial or polynomial-like. Knowing a model's zeros tells you where a quantity hits zero (break-even, impact, equilibrium); knowing its end behavior tells you whether it's a reasonable model far outside the data you collected it from, which matters anytime you're tempted to extrapolate."
    }
  ],
